WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.362
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.257
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.557 --> 00:00:17.318
Unit seven: Postmodern Criticism. Today you will discuss abstract literary ideas with dense nominal groups.

4
00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:20.166
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.414
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.483
Postmodernism.

7
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:33.163
Postmodernism means a late-20th-century movement sceptical of grand narratives and fixed meaning.

8
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.876
Postmodernism questions the idea of a single truth.

9
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:40.147
Your turn. Say: Postmodernism.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.257
Postmodernism.

11
00:00:45.017 --> 00:00:46.285
Fragmentation.

12
00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:51.884
Fragmentation means the breaking of a text or experience into separate pieces.

13
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:55.655
The fragmentation of the plot mirrors the hero's confusion.

14
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.471
Your turn. Say: Fragmentation.

15
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:03.567
Fragmentation.

16
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:05.341
Metafiction.

17
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.474
Metafiction means fiction that draws attention to its own status as fiction.

18
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:14.650
The novel becomes metafiction when the author appears as a character.

19
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.053
Your turn. Say: Metafiction.

20
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:21.971
Metafiction.

21
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:23.706
Pastiche.

22
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:28.411
Pastiche means a work that imitates or mixes earlier styles.

23
00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:32.816
The film is a pastiche of 1950s thrillers.

24
00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:36.185
Your turn. Say: Pastiche.

25
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:39.895
Pastiche.

26
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:41.512
Irony.

27
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:46.142
Irony means a gap between surface meaning and intended meaning.

28
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.481
Postmodern irony makes sincerity difficult.

29
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:52.480
Your turn. Say: Irony.

30
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:56.300
Irony.

31
00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.248
Deconstruction.

32
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:03.537
Deconstruction means a method that exposes hidden contradictions in a text.

33
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.891
Deconstruction shows how the text undermines its own claims.

34
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:11.438
Your turn. Say: Deconstruction.

35
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.615
Deconstruction.

36
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.456
Ambiguity.

37
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.436
Ambiguity means openness to several meanings.

38
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.955
The deliberate ambiguity resists a single reading.

39
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.318
Your turn. Say: Ambiguity.

40
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.261
Ambiguity.

41
00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:34.296
Self-reference.

42
00:02:35.283 --> 00:02:38.409
Self-reference means a text referring to itself.

43
00:02:39.017 --> 00:02:41.562
The book's self-reference reminds us we are reading.

44
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.046
Your turn. Say: Self-reference.

45
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.004
Self-reference.

46
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.532
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.383 --> 00:03:00.184
Visal, the novel you chose keeps interrupting itself. Isn't that just confusing?

48
00:03:01.283 --> 00:03:10.472
It is deliberate. The fragmentation of the narrative reflects the uncertainty of modern life, where no single story explains everything.

49
00:03:11.583 --> 00:03:14.535
And the moment when the author addresses the reader directly?

50
00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:24.351
That's metafiction. The constant self-reference forces us to recognise the artificiality of all storytelling.

51
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.576
Some critics say postmodernism is just clever games.

52
00:03:29.683 --> 00:03:39.998
There is a danger of empty irony, I admit. But at its best, the questioning of grand narratives exposes who has the power to tell stories.

53
00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:42.657
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:43.517 --> 00:03:45.969
Grammar focus: Nominalization.

55
00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:47.398
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.083 --> 00:03:51.418
The fragmentation of the narrative reflects modern uncertainty.

57
00:03:52.317 --> 00:03:56.766
The constant self-reference of the text exposes the artificiality of storytelling.

58
00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.950
The questioning of grand narratives reveals who holds power.

59
00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:11.913
In criticism, nominal groups can be long: determiner + adjective + noun + 'of' phrase. Keep the main verb close to the head noun so the reader does not get lost.

60
00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.473
Be careful! Don't say: The fragmentate of the plot reflects chaos.

61
00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:23.256
Say: The fragmentation of the plot reflects chaos.

62
00:04:23.556 --> 00:04:25.799
Use the noun form: fragmentation.

63
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.009
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.695
After the Grand Narratives.

65
00:04:31.995 --> 00:04:38.209
Postmodern criticism emerged from a growing distrust of 'grand narratives' that claim to explain all of history.

66
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:46.807
Its favoured techniques, among them pastiche, metafiction and fragmentation, deliberately disrupt the reader's expectations.

67
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.946
The refusal of a fixed meaning is not mere playfulness; it is a challenge to authority.

68
00:04:53.933 --> 00:04:59.636
Deconstruction, for example, reveals how texts depend on the very oppositions they claim to reject.

69
00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:04.943
Critics argue, however, that relentless irony can leave readers with nothing to believe in.

70
00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:09.687
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.480
Choose the best nominal phrase.

72
00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:20.195
A, the narrative's fragmenting of itself. B, the fragmentation of the narrative. C, the narrative fragmentationly.

73
00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:27.114
The answer is B: the fragmentation of the narrative.

74
00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:31.643
Fiction that reminds readers it is fiction is called blank.

75
00:05:31.943 --> 00:05:35.701
A, metafiction. B, pastiche. C, allegory.

76
00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.522
The answer is A: metafiction.

77
00:05:42.683 --> 00:05:45.925
A film that imitates several old styles is a blank.

78
00:05:46.225 --> 00:05:50.366
A, paradigm. B, deconstruction. C, pastiche.

79
00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.865
The answer is C: pastiche.

80
00:05:57.017 --> 00:05:58.854
Which collocation is natural?

81
00:05:59.154 --> 00:06:04.665
A, expose a contradiction. B, do a contradiction. C, make up a contradiction.

82
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.664
The answer is A: expose a contradiction.

83
00:06:11.817 --> 00:06:15.372
According to the reading, what do critics say about relentless irony?

84
00:06:15.672 --> 00:06:22.390
A, It explains all of history.. B, It can leave readers with nothing to believe in.. C, It strengthens authority..

85
00:06:25.933 --> 00:06:29.581
The answer is B: It can leave readers with nothing to believe in..

86
00:06:30.767 --> 00:06:33.965
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:34.817 --> 00:06:37.722
What is one example of metafiction you have encountered?

88
00:06:54.517 --> 00:06:56.005
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:56.305 --> 00:07:27.131
One clear example of metafiction I have encountered is a film in which a character suddenly looks at the camera and speaks to the audience. The breaking of the illusion made me laugh, but it also made me think about how stories manipulate us. In some novels, too, the narrator admits that he is inventing the events. A few viewers find this distracting. Personally, I think it encourages a more critical awareness of every story we consume.

90
00:07:27.431 --> 00:07:29.199
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:30.083 --> 00:07:33.862
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and nominalization.

92
00:07:34.162 --> 00:07:40.030
Today's value is critical awareness. Use technology wisely, and always think for yourself.

93
00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.890
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.552
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

